{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":257718,"duration_api_ms":270737,"num_turns":28,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"b63547b0-92f4-4197-8a33-aa1e693346fe","total_cost_usd":1.5190955000000004,"usage":{"input_tokens":22,"cache_creation_input_tokens":82857,"cache_read_input_tokens":1335365,"output_tokens":6572,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":82857},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":22,"outputTokens":6572,"cacheReadInputTokens":1335365,"cacheCreationInputTokens":82857,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.3499487499999998,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000},"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":1159,"outputTokens":7672,"cacheReadInputTokens":751890,"cacheCreationInputTokens":43551,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.16914675000000004,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"amendments":[{"body":"In calibration.rs, `classify_f64` does not validate that `min <= max`. An inverted range (min > max) causes ALL values to silently classify as Fail because the bounds check `value < min || value > max` is satisfied for every input when the range is inverted. Add a `debug_assert!(min <= max)` at the top of `classify_f64` (and by extension `classify_u32`) to catch this class of bug during testing. The Envelope builder methods currently pass min/max in the correct order, so this is a latent bug, not a live one — but the calibration module is public API meant for external use, and an inverted range would produce confusing all-fail results with no diagnostic.","rationale":"Silent incorrect behavior on inverted ranges is a robustness hazard for a public calibration API. A debug_assert is zero-cost in release and provides immediate feedback during development. Since envelope construction is done manually by test authors, a typo swapping min/max would be very hard to diagnose without this guard."},{"body":"In calibration.rs `CalibrationRun::finish()`, when `overall_status != CheckStatus::Pass`, artifacts are written twice: first via `self.inner.persist_artifacts()` on line 338, then again inside `self.inner.finish()` on line 345 (because `check_envelope` records failures in the inner harness via `record_failure`, so `finish()` sees non-empty failures and calls `write_artifacts` again). The second write overwrites the first. Consider either (a) not calling `persist_artifacts` separately and letting `finish` handle it, or (b) calling a consume-without-artifacts method if one exists. This is a minor inefficiency, not a correctness bug.","rationale":"Double artifact writes are wasteful (disk I/O for JSON serialization twice) and confusing to someone reading the code — they'd expect `persist_artifacts` to be the definitive write, but `finish` silently overwrites it. Simplifying the flow would improve maintainability."},{"body":"In calibration.rs lines 76-77, the doc comment \"Result of checking a single field against its envelope bounds.\" is duplicated verbatim on consecutive lines above the `FieldCheck` struct. Remove the duplicate line.","rationale":"Copy-paste artifact that's trivial to fix. While cosmetic, it could confuse doc generation or readers into thinking something was accidentally left behind."}],"summary":"Reviewed all changed files: calibration.rs (741 lines, new), lib.rs (2 lines added for module export), calibration_report.rs (407 lines, new test file), and e2e_shrimp_scenario_probes.rs (9 lines changed). All code compiles cleanly. All 13 tests pass (6 scenario probes + 7 calibration report tests). The five acceptance criteria are fully met: all 5 shrimp scenarios have named test functions with husbandry doc comments; failures dump comprehensive per-stage shrimp state and water chemistry via A2d tracing; the e2e summary test runs all 5 scenarios with structured traces and exits non-zero on violation; assertions use directional/envelope comparisons throughout; the breeding scenario runs 1440 hours (60 days) with an explicit 2+ cycle assertion. Found three issues: (1) classify_f64 lacks a debug_assert for inverted min>max ranges — latent correctness hazard for a public API; (2) CalibrationRun::finish double-writes artifacts for failing scenarios; (3) duplicate doc comment on FieldCheck struct. No safety or data-loss concerns."},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"8897a214-6af7-4760-ab68-f75d840b74fc"}
